From e6559e05cc39f2e66ee3360214a1758c5fcf32a4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: elliotttate <elliotttate@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 18:54:43 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Smooth Motion: only meshes whose positions the game wrote
 blended vertex by vertex (the Mac's water path, at its old cost)

The water change (0131) blended every small indexed mesh without position
matrix indices vertex by vertex: each kept its own vertex range, so none
was batched, and each copied its decoded vertices for the helper. Nearly
all of them are the world's static models, whose positions never change, so
the blend found nothing to do. On all of the i9's cores with Smooth Motion at
60 that cost 1.5-2.5 ms of GX worker and 1.5-2.5 ms of render worker time per
game frame at Forest Haven, Hyrule, Hyrule Castle and Dragon Roost.

Now positions_written() hashes each position array's bytes once a game
frame (the widest span its draws read) and remembers the frame they last
changed in. Only an array written in this frame or the one before, or met
for the first time, makes its draws blend vertex by vertex; the rest are
matched by matrices and batched as before 0131. Draws with direct UVs (the
sea's grid) and direct positions keep the 0131 path. Keys still follow the
topology (may_blend_vertices()), so a mesh starting or stopping keeps its
key. The guest's dirty epochs were tried first: they miss small meshes the
game writes without a cache flush (the Forsaken Fortress's flag).

Smooth Motion dumps against 201e909: Outset 62 of 62 and the sea by the
Forsaken Fortress 42 of 42 identical. CPU per game frame back to ef3e17f's
within run-to-run noise at six places. DOL_AURORA_INTERP_ALL_VERTICES=1 gives
0131's behaviour; DOL_AURORA_INTERP_VERTEX_LOG=1 logs arrays and writes.
